WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.777 --> 00:00:15.777
(instrumental music)

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:19.150
- [Narrator] Most people think
it's a food safety issue.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:21.651
You keep the oyster
alive as long as possible

00:00:21.651 --> 00:00:25.420
and that reduces the risk
of bacterial contamination,

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.760
and there is a little
bit of truth to that.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:31.300
Oysters can carry a scary
flesh-eating bacteria called

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.020
Vibrio vulnificus.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:34.390
You can get it from oysters

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:38.080
or from swimming with an
open wound in brackish water

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:39.940
where the bacteria lives.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:42.330
But, let's put things in prospective.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:46.810
- The risk of running into a
bad oyster is phenomenally low.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:48.830
My biggest pet peeve is people like,

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.660
go crazy over one bad oyster in the news,

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.880
but they don't really care
that hundreds of thousands

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:57.110
of pounds of lettuce are
contaminated with Salmonella.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:58.380
- [Narrator] About 100 people die

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.819
from Vibrio infections each year.

00:01:00.819 --> 00:01:05.480
About 450 die from Salmonella.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:09.330
Plus, the FDA requires that
oyster farm have to test

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.540
water quality before sending oysters

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.420
out to markets and restaurants,

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:18.140
and that's important because
oysters are filter feeders,

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:21.470
they soak basically anything
that's in the water around them

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:25.640
including fecal matter which
can come from rain runoff.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:29.460
Yak, but there's a clever
little secret way you can check

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.340
how fresh your oysters are.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:35.030
- One thing that you can
ask for is a shellfish tag,

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:38.546
which every, retailer or
restaurant is required to have

00:01:38.546 --> 00:01:42.120
every bag of oyster that they
purchase for up to 90 days,

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:46.320
after that purchase. So, that
tag, if they don't have it,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:47.670
don't eat those oysters.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:49.390
- [Narrator] This tag
is a way for restaurant

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:53.390
to track where and when
the oysters were farmed.

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:56.020
Qiu says that she looks
for the most recent dates

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.190
on the tag, anything
further out than two weeks

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:02.210
won't taste this good,
and increases the risk

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:03.630
of a bad oyster.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.120
Some chefs may look at you funny

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.110
for asking for this documentation,

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:10.150
but it's a strategy that apparently works.

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:13.026
- I tried to do the
math and I probably had

00:02:13.026 --> 00:02:18.026
over six or seven thousand
oysters by now in my lifetime

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.700
and I've never gotten
sick once from an oyster.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.830
- [Narrator] Basically, oysters are safe.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:27.953
So, question, why on earth
are they still sometimes alive

00:02:27.953 --> 00:02:30.980
or dying when we're eating them?

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:34.450
- You really want you're
raw shellfish to be

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:39.450
absolutely fresh and, you
know, the freshest you can get

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.980
is something that is just
very recently killed.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:47.240
So, it goes back to not
only the food safety

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.550
but the actual taste and
the texture of that oyster

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.640
to me just far superior.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.740
- [Narrator] So, basically
freshly killed oysters

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.030
taste better, and it's
hard to tell exactly

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:03.030
when an oyster dies, because
before it's served it's shocked

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:06.070
and shocking is, how should I put this,

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:09.390
shocking is not a gentle process.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:12.700
Shocking involves separating
the oyster abductor muscle

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:16.310
form its shell, this muscle
gives the oyster control

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:18.710
over opening and closing the shell,

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:22.460
similar to how your spinal
cord helps you move.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:26.018
So, severing their abductor
muscle is almost like,

00:03:26.018 --> 00:03:29.780
severing you spine. Yikes!

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:33.200
Most restaurants in the US
keep their oysters alive on ice

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.860
up until the shocking process,
which either kills the oyster

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.880
or renders it completely immobile.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.040
Since they don't move around
much in the first place

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.823
it's kind of hard to tell which.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.770
So it's easy to feel guilty
sitting there, eating an oyster

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:52.363
that was either just
killed or is maybe dying.

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:58.250
But consider the oyster biology.

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:00.920
It's very primitive, so it's possible

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:03.070
they might not even feel pain at all.

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:04.470
- [Julie Qiu] They don't have a brain,

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:06.870
they're not really processing
pain in the same way

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:10.220
that we process, any kind of feeling,

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:14.250
so, I don't believe that
they are feeling pain

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.120
in the same way that
we are thinking of it.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.587
- [Narrator] So, really it's a up to you,

00:04:18.587 --> 00:04:20.900
if you don't wanna eat
oyster, that's fine,

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:24.150
and if you do you won't be the first.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:28.170
- It's one of the few foods
that have not changed in like,

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:30.420
thousands and thousands of years.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:33.640
So, being able to appreciate
a food that has remained

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:37.070
unchanged for that long is
something really special

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:39.090
and remarkable and I think
it should be celebrated

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:40.614
for what it is.

00:04:40.614 --> 00:04:45.614
(instrumental music)

